
01. NUMBER OF SUBARRAYS WITH SUM K


1. Problem Statement

Given an integer array and a target k, count the number of non-empty contiguous subarrays whose sum equals k. The array may contain negative numbers.

Example:
Input: nums = [1, -1, 1], k = 1
Output: 3

2. Brute-Force Approach

Try every starting index and extend the subarray while maintaining its sum. Whenever the sum equals k, increase the count.

Code:

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
using namespace std;

int subarraySumBrute(vector<int>& nums, int k) {
    int count = 0;

    for (int i = 0; i < nums.size(); i++) {
        int sum = 0;

        for (int j = i; j < nums.size(); j++) {
            sum += nums[j];

            if (sum == k) {
                count++;
            }
        }
    }

    return count;
}

Complexity:
Time: O(n^2)
Auxiliary Space: O(1)

3. Optimized Approach: Prefix Sum + Frequency Map

If an earlier prefix sum equals the current sum minus k, the elements between them form a subarray with sum k.

Code:

#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <unordered_map>
using namespace std;

long long subarraySum(vector<int>& nums, long long k) {
    unordered_map<long long, long long> freq;
    freq[0] = 1;

    long long sum = 0;
    long long count = 0;

    for (int x : nums) {
        sum += x;
        count += freq[sum - k];
        freq[sum]++;
    }

    return count;
}

int main() {
    vector<int> nums = {1, -1, 1};
    cout << subarraySum(nums, 1);
    return 0;
}

Complexity:
Expected Time: O(n)
Auxiliary Space: O(n)

4. Why This Works

We store the frequency of every prefix sum. This allows us to count valid subarrays without checking every possible subarray.

We initialize freq[0] = 1 to count subarrays that start at index zero.

5. Edge Cases

Empty array -> 0
[5], k = 5 -> 1
[0, 0, 0], k = 0 -> 6
[1, -1, 1], k = 1 -> 3
[1, 2, 3], k = 7 -> 0
